 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
 - **Precision:** Near the boundary where $S_b(p) \approx -S_a(p)$, floating-point precision errors can cause artifacts. Ensure epsilon thresholds in ray marching are appropriate for the scale of the scene [G].
-- **Degenerate Input:** If the subtractor completely contains the base, the resulting SDF will be positive everywhere (empty solid). If they do not intersect, the result is simply the base SDF where the subtractor is outside, and the inverted subtractor where it overlaps [G].
-- **Normals:** The normal of the resulting surface is derived from the gradient of the resulting SDF. At the boundary where the subtractor cuts the base, the normal is determined by the base. At the boundary created by the subtractor's interior (the hole), the normal is determined by the subtractor (inverted) [G].
